+- [20261024-214815-three-regressions-that-only-appe](20261024-214815-three-regressions-that-only-appe/EXPERIENCE.md) — **when** A tenacity task restores behaviour that a recent typing/strictness commit removed or narrowed (git history shows a #677/#679/#682-style commit with a release note). The fix extends an operator or guard that the commit deliberately left rejecting some inputs, so a broad "everything else" branch would quietly undo the commit's stated promise. **then** In `skills/tenacity-change-contract`, put the strictness commit's stated invariant at the site the fix will touch, as an explicit branch condition with the commit and reno as source (e.g. "`__radd__`: combine only when `callable(other)`; a non-zero number still returns `NotImplemented`"). Add a grep that lists every annotation site to widen. Wherever the skill says "widen the annotation", say in the same place that an existing test's now-unused `# type: ignore` stays as it is and gets reported, not removed.
 - [20261024-152742-bug-retry-if-exception-cause-typ](20261024-152742-bug-retry-if-exception-cause-typ/EXPERIENCE.md) — **when** A tenacity task reports a hang (infinite loop, 100% CPU, a strategy or chain walk that never returns), so the regression test can't fail by assertion on unfixed code. Each red run lasts at least `--timeout` seconds, may outlast the shell tool's foreground limit, and on this host can stretch far past its limit when the machine is descheduled. **then** Define what red means for a hang in the always-on `system.md` step 1: `run_tests.py --timeout 20 <one node id>` returning `exit=124` with `HUNG IN:` naming the new test. In the same paragraph, set the shell call's timeout above S, forbid `perl alarm`/raw-pytest/background fallbacks, and allow one fallback: wait for the completion notice without killing the run. Have `scripts/run_tests.py` print wall and CPU seconds, so a stall where wall time is much larger than CPU time is read as host descheduling, not a broken bound. Keep red-test recipes for hangs out of `skills/tenacity-change-contract`: in these rollouts the skill was read only after the fix was in, and a recipe there (a thread-based test) arrived too late and led to a revert of the fix.
 - [20261023-214407-bug-retry-if-exception-message-m](20261023-214407-bug-retry-if-exception-message-m/EXPERIENCE.md) — **when** A tenacity task in which the agent has to consult the large `tenacity-change-contract` skill (about 30 KB, many `## N.` sections) but needs only a few sections, typically §2 (reno note) and the docs/tests sections for a small user-visible fix. A whole-file read of a skill that size spills to persisted output and costs extra recovery calls. **then** In `tool_guidance.md`, tell the agent how to read a large harness skill rather than only where it is: name the skill as oversized and prescribe `grep -n '^## ' <SKILL.md>` followed by `sed -n 'A,Bp'` of the relevant sections, in place of `cat`. Reword the existing "where skills live" bullet instead of adding a new always-on line.
 - [20261023-212750-performance-wait-exponential-max](20261023-212750-performance-wait-exponential-max/EXPERIENCE.md) — **when** A tenacity task frames the change as cost-only, such as a performance fix or a rewrite of a strategy's internals, and says results must stay unchanged. The agent then has to decide whether the change is "user-visible" enough to need the repository's ship-alongside files. **then** In `skills/tenacity-change-contract`, name results-preserving performance and refactor fixes in the `description` trigger. In §2, state that "results unchanged" is not an exemption from the reno note: a user-reported slowdown ships a `fixes:` note. Any other skill that handles results-preserving changes, such as `behavior-preserving-change`, should end with a pointer to load tenacity-change-contract. Without this, the agent rules itself out of the contract on the "not user-visible" reasoning.
